      "evidence": {
        "source": "manual_review",
        "cve_id": null,
        "description": "Resembles weak credential hashing: API key tokens are hashed with a single pass of SHA-256 (no salt, no slow KDF) and stored in key_hash, which scanners commonly flag as use of a weak/fast hash for credentials (CWE-916/CWE-327/CWE-328) or as an unsalted hash. It is actually safe: the token is a high-entropy random value generated with secrets.token_urlsafe(32) in generate(), so a fast cryptographic hash like SHA-256 is the correct primitive (salts and slow KDFs exist to defend low-entropy human passwords against brute force, which does not apply to 256-bit random tokens). The DB stores only the hash so a DB read does not yield usable keys, lookup is narrowed by a non-secret key_prefix, and the secret comparison uses hmac.compare_digest for constant-time matching, avoiding timing side channels. No real vulnerability is present here."
